  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">When to use it</p>
    <h2>Match your drinking plan to the actual situation</h2>
    <p>Everyday drinking, a short workout, prolonged endurance exercise, a hot work shift, illness and clinician-directed fluid replacement are not the same hydration problem.</p>
    <p>For routine use, drink according to thirst, meals, activity, environment and the current product label. During prolonged heat or heavy sweating, fluid and electrolyte needs can rise substantially. In clinical situations, medical conditions, medications and fluid or sodium restrictions may change what is safe.</p>
    <p>Best Hydrate can be one prepared beverage within that broader strategy. It should not replace urgent assessment when someone has signs of significant heat illness, severe fluid loss or inability to keep fluids down.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Preparation discipline</p>
  <h2>Three common mistakes to avoid</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article><h3>Doubling powder by feel</h3><p>Concentrating a drink changes its carbohydrate and electrolyte concentration. Follow the measured serving instead of assuming stronger is better.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Treating one serving as a universal fluid prescription</h3><p>Total fluid needs vary with body size, food intake, weather, activity, sweat rate, illness and individual health.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Using a consumer hydration mix as emergency treatment</h3><p>Severe dehydration, heat illness, persistent vomiting, altered consciousness or other concerning symptoms require appropriate medical assessment.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Heat and long work</p>
    <h2>Frequent access matters more than one large catch-up drink</h2>
    <p>Current NIOSH guidance for moderate work in heat lasting less than two hours recommends about one cup of water every 15 to 20 minutes, with electrolyte-containing drinks considered when sweating continues for several hours. That occupational guidance is a heat-safety framework, not a Best Hydrate dosing instruction.</p>
    <p>For long shifts and heavy sweating, the practical lesson is to plan fluid access, breaks and cooling in advance rather than relying on a single drink after symptoms start.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Important</span>
    <h3>Water, rest and cooling still matter.</h3>
    <p>An electrolyte drink cannot compensate for unsafe heat exposure, inadequate rest or lack of access to cool fluids.</p>
  </aside>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Storage and handling</p>
  <h2>Keep preparation simple and hygienic</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article><h3>Keep powder dry</h3><p>Close the container after use and follow storage instructions on the current label. Avoid introducing wet utensils or moisture into the jar.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Use clean drinkware</h3><p>Wash bottles and lids regularly, especially if prepared drinks sit warm for long periods.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Check the package</h3><p>Use the lot, expiry, caution and preparation information printed on the product you actually have.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

